# Impulse firmware configuration
# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

menu "Impulse firmware"

choice IMPULSE_ROLE
	prompt "Device role"
	default IMPULSE_ROLE_WATCH
	help
	  One PCB, two roles, two images. Selected at COMPILE time, never at
	  runtime — firmware_spec_v3_nrf.md §1.3. The watch is the root of
	  trust; a runtime-selectable role would mean a watch could be told it
	  is an anchor, and an anchor does not enforce. That would reduce the
	  entire commitment-integrity chapter to a single settings write.

config IMPULSE_ROLE_WATCH
	bool "Watch"

config IMPULSE_ROLE_ANCHOR
	bool "Anchor"

endchoice

config IMPULSE_BLE
	bool "App-facing BLE peripheral and GATT surface"
	default y
	select BT
	select BT_PERIPHERAL
	select BT_GATT_DYNAMIC_DB
	help
	  The watch GATT service of firmware_spec_v2.md §5.6, minus the
	  deletions in v3 §4.8. This is how the app pushes a schedule, so with
	  it off the device cannot be configured at all.

	  The contract is FROZEN and declared in four places (CLAUDE.md).
	  Changing a UUID or a payload here without moving the app and
	  phone_sim in the same change breaks the product.

config IMPULSE_NET
	bool "WiFi + UDP anchor escalation (WATCH_REMOVED)"
	depends on WIFI_NRF70
	default y if WIFI_NRF70
	help
	  The §5.5.1 anchor-escalation path: the watch tells anchors it has been
	  taken off, over UDP. This is the transport that closes Sunrise Lock's
	  known bypass — leave the watch out of BLE range and go back to bed —
	  so it deliberately rides WiFi rather than BLE, which by construction
	  cannot outreach the thing it is trying to detect the absence of.

config IMPULSE_ESCALATION_WIFI_GIVEUP_MIN
	int "Minutes to keep retrying WiFi escalation before giving up"
	default 10
	help
	  How long the watch keeps trying to reach its anchors over WiFi after
	  the BLE link has failed, before it stops.

	  Escalation is BLE-first: the watch already holds a connection to the
	  anchor for ranging, so it writes there. WiFi is the fallback for the
	  case that matters most — the watch carried out of BLE range and left
	  there, which is Sunrise Lock's known bypass. Those two are mutually
	  exclusive by construction, which is required: BLE and WiFi cannot both
	  run on Board V1 (agent-notes 2026-09-12), and the FCC grant is
	  expected to forbid simultaneous operation.

	  Giving up is a DELIBERATE limit, not a fix. A watch that can reach
	  neither its anchor nor any network cannot escalate at all, and no
	  amount of retrying changes that; it is out of scope by decision.

config IMPULSE_CS_REFLECTOR
	bool "Channel Sounding REFLECTOR (anchor role)"
	help
	  The anchor half of the ranging pair: answers an initiator's tones and
	  serves ranging data over RAS. Computes no distance — the watch does
	  that, because the watch is the root of trust and an anchor is not
	  trusted to report its own distance (v3 §4).

	  Mutually exclusive with IMPULSE_CS_BACKEND, which is the initiator.
	  Build with: ./build.sh anchor -- -DEXTRA_CONF_FILE="anchor.conf;cs_reflector.conf"

config IMPULSE_CS_BACKEND
	bool "Real Channel Sounding backend (BLE + RAS)"
	default n
	select BT
	select BT_CENTRAL
	select BT_SMP
	select BT_GATT_CLIENT
	select BT_GATT_DYNAMIC_DB
	select BT_CHANNEL_SOUNDING
	select BT_RAS
	select BT_RAS_RREQ
	select BT_SCAN
	select BT_SCAN_FILTER_ENABLE
	select BT_CS_DE
	select BT_TRANSMIT_POWER_CONTROL
	select FPU
	select FPU_SHARING
	select CBPRINTF_FP_SUPPORT
	help
	  Replaces the abstaining stub with the real radio. Verified working
	  between Board V1 and an nRF54L15-DK in ../nrf_cs_test.

	  Default n while it still owns its own central connection and ranges
	  against whatever advertises the RAS service rather than against
	  event.anchorId — see the gap list at the top of cs_backend_nrf.c.
	  With it off, every measurement abstains, which fails CLOSED for
	  stayNear and open for getAway, exactly as spec §4.5 requires.

config IMPULSE_BRINGUP_SHELL
	bool "Bring-up shell over RTT"
	depends on SHELL
	default y
	help
	  Interactive commands that drive the buzzer, motor and LED ring
	  directly. Board V1 has no UART, so this is the only way to poke a
	  driver by hand.

	  MUST BE OFF IN A RELEASE IMAGE. On a commitment device, a runtime
	  path that can silence the motor and buzzer is a bypass; this is the
	  same reasoning that makes write-capable debug tools compile-time
	  excluded in the app rather than runtime-gated.

config IMPULSE_BUZZER_DISABLED
	bool "Compile out ALL buzzer output"
	default y
	help
	  Makes it impossible for this build to make a sound. Intended for
	  hardware testing in public, where an enforcement alarm or an anchor
	  removal beep going off would be a genuine disruption.

	  This is COMPILE-TIME on purpose, and it is belt and braces:
	    - impulse_buzzer_set() compiles to nothing, so no code path — not
	      an enforcement profile, not the anchor beep pattern, not the
	      Identify characteristic, not the bring-up shell — can drive it;
	    - the buzzer pin is never configured as an output. It is left as a
	      high-impedance input, so even a stray register write cannot make
	      the pad drive. R17 (10k to GND) holds the net low regardless.

	  A runtime flag would not do: on a commitment device the whole point
	  of the buzzer is that the user cannot silence it in the moment, so
	  the only safe way to guarantee silence is to not build the code.

	  DEFAULT IS y — REMEMBER TO SET IT BACK. The firmware prints a loud
	  warning at every boot while this is on, precisely so a silent buzzer
	  is never mistaken for a hardware fault later.

config IMPULSE_ADC_CH_BATTERY
	int "SAADC channel carrying the battery divider"
	default 0
	help
	  IDENTIFIED ON HARDWARE 2026-08-30, not read from a datasheet: the
	  AIN-to-pin mapping for the nRF54LM20A is in none of our documents
	  (BM20C.pdf lists analog functions only for the BM15). A boot sweep of
	  all eight inputs found AIN0 sitting at 2069 mV — Vbat/2 for a 4.14 V
	  cell — while every other channel was at rail, at zero, or noise.

	  This part has EIGHT inputs, AIN0..AIN7. Higher ids are rejected by
	  the driver.

config IMPULSE_ADC_CH_IR
	int "SAADC channel carrying the IR worn-detect receiver"
	default 1
	help
	  Also identified by the boot sweep: AIN1 was the only channel that
	  moved when the IR emitter was toggled (137 mV -> 73 mV). The swing is
	  NEGATIVE because the ITR8307's phototransistor pulls the node down as
	  it receives light, so more reflection means a lower reading.

config IMPULSE_WORN_THRESHOLD_MV
	int "IR lit-vs-ambient difference to become WORN"
	default 200
	help
	  Worn detection is lit-minus-ambient (§5.2), never an absolute level —
	  an absolute threshold tracks room lighting rather than skin.

	  STILL AN ESTIMATE. It could not be measured from a wrist because the
	  IR window is covered by the enclosure (2026-09-10), so this is set
	  from the NO-WRIST side only, which is the half that can be measured:

	    bare board, nothing in front of the sensor .... 64 mV
	    board in the case, on a desk, 8 samples ....... 75-90 mV

	  The case RAISES the floor — the plastic reflects the emitter straight
	  back — so the crosstalk this must clear is ~90 mV, not 64. 200 mV is
	  a bit over 2x that.

	  BIASED HIGH ON PURPOSE. The two failure directions are not equal:
	  reading "not worn" while worn costs a donning grace and a spurious
	  WATCH_REMOVED, while reading "worn" on a nightstand tells the system
	  a commitment is being honoured by a watch nobody is wearing. The
	  second is the one worth engineering against.

	  IF WORN NEVER ASSERTS, this is why — the number is too high for what
	  the enclosure lets through, not a broken sensor. Measure a real wrist
	  through the final window and set both this and the release value
	  below. See HARDWARE_TEST_PLAN.

config IMPULSE_WORN_RELEASE_MV
	int "IR difference at which WORN is released"
	default 140
	help
	  Hysteresis. Below this the watch is considered off the wrist; between
	  this and IMPULSE_WORN_THRESHOLD_MV the previous state is held.

	  There was previously ONE threshold, so a delta resting near it would
	  chatter worn/not-worn every second — and each flip drives the donning
	  grace (§5.4.4) and WATCH_REMOVED, which are not free. Must stay above
	  the measured no-wrist ceiling (~90 mV) and below the worn threshold.

config IMPULSE_MAX_EVENTS
	int "Maximum events in a stored schedule"
	default 64
	help
	  Bounds the deserialised schedule. The ESP32 build cut this to 24 under
	  heap pressure on a different MCU; this part has far more RAM, so the
	  original 64 is restored. A blob declaring more events than this is
	  rejected rather than truncated — a silently shortened schedule is a
	  silently weakened commitment.

config IMPULSE_SYSWORKQ_WATCHDOG
	bool "Reboot if the system workqueue stops making progress"
	default y
	help
	  Supervises the SYSTEM workqueue from the main thread and cold-reboots
	  the device if it stops running work.

	  This exists because of a real deadlock, diagnosed on the anchor over
	  SWD on 2026-09-12 (agent-notes). On a BLE disconnect the host runs its
	  connection teardown on the system workqueue; for a Channel Sounding
	  link that reaches bt_ras_rrsp_free(), which calls
	  k_work_queue_drain(&rrsp_wq) and waits K_FOREVER. k_work_cancel()
	  just above it does NOT stop an already-RUNNING handler, so if the RAS
	  reflector was mid-send it keeps running and blocks forever waiting for
	  an ATT buffer that a dead connection will never return. The system
	  workqueue is then wedged for good.

	  Nothing about that is visible from the outside: the main loop, the
	  logger and the heartbeat all keep running on their own threads, so the
	  device looks perfectly healthy while being unreachable over BLE and
	  unable to run any deferred work at all. It cost two days of "the
	  anchor keeps dying while powered" before the thread states were read.

	  A cold reboot is the honest response — the connection object is leaked
	  (ref never drops) and no in-process recovery can reclaim it. The
	  schedule is in NVS and the clock is persisted, so a reboot is cheap.

config IMPULSE_SYSWORKQ_STALL_S
	int "Seconds without system-workqueue progress before rebooting"
	depends on IMPULSE_SYSWORKQ_WATCHDOG
	default 60
	help
	  Must be comfortably longer than any legitimate work item. The probe is
	  submitted every quarter of this interval, so the true detection window
	  is between 0.75x and 1x this value.

config IMPULSE_MAX_EVENTS_PER_DAY
	int "Maximum events resolved for a single day"
	default 32

config IMPULSE_PENDING_QUEUE_MAX
	int "Maximum quarantined loosening changes"
	default 16
	help
	  firmware_spec_v2.md §9.10 PENDING_QUEUE_MAX. When full, further
	  loosenings are DROPPED, not applied.

endmenu

